{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:3.297", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "3.297", "chapter": "3.297", "section": "Yakṣapraśna"}, "paraphrase": "At the lake the Yakṣa questions Yudhiṣṭhira in riddles — what makes the sun rise, what is heavier than the earth (the mother) and higher than the sky (the father), what is the best of gains (health), of happiness (contentment), what is the highest dharma — and Yudhiṣṭhira answers each.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "ultimate"], "teachers": ["tch:yudhisthira", "tch:dharma"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:3.297.55", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "3.297.55", "chapter": "3.297", "verse": "55", "section": "Yakṣapraśna"}, "paraphrase": "Non-cruelty is the highest dharma; the dharma of the three Vedas always bears fruit; the mind restrained, people do not grieve; friendship with the good does not decay.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "speaker": "Yudhiṣṭhira", "addressee": "the Yakṣa (Dharma)", "terms": ["trm:anrsamsya", "trm:dharma"], "concepts": ["cpt:non-violence"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Verse ref checked against the local digitized text (Critical Edition).", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:3.297.63-64", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "3.297.63-64", "chapter": "3.297", "verse": "63-64", "section": "Yakṣapraśna"}, "paraphrase": "Yudhiṣṭhira: as long as the sound of a person's good deeds touches heaven and earth, so long is he called a person; the one to whom the pleasant and unpleasant, pleasure and pain, past and future are alike is the man who possesses all wealth.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "consciousness-mind"], "concepts": ["cpt:equanimity"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Verse ref checked against the local digitized text (Critical Edition).", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:3.297.66-73", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "3.297.66-73", "chapter": "3.297", "verse": "66-73", "section": "Yakṣapraśna"}, "paraphrase": "Allowed to have one brother revived, Yudhiṣṭhira chooses Nakula, son of Mādrī, over the mighty Bhīma and Arjuna: non-cruelty is the highest dharma; as Kuntī is, so is Mādrī to him, and he wishes both mothers to have a living son.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "terms": ["trm:anrsamsya"], "concepts": ["cpt:subtlety-of-dharma"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Verse ref checked against the local digitized text (Critical Edition).", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:3.298", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "3.298", "chapter": "3.298", "section": "Yakṣapraśna"}, "paraphrase": "The Yakṣa reveals himself as Dharma, Yudhiṣṭhira's father, revives all the brothers, and grants boons.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "divine", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission"], "teachers": ["tch:dharma"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:vulg-3.313/1", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "vulg-3.313/1", "chapter": "vulg-3.313", "section": "Yakṣapraśna (vulgate only)"}, "paraphrase": "(Vulgate) Asked what is the greatest wonder, Yudhiṣṭhira answers: day after day beings go to the abode of Yama, yet those who remain wish to live for ever — what could be more wonderful?", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["death-dying"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Not in the critical text of 3.297–298 (checked); Gita Press Vanaparvan 313 — verse number not confirmed.", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:vulg-3.313/2", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "vulg-3.313/2", "chapter": "vulg-3.313", "section": "Yakṣapraśna (vulgate only)"}, "paraphrase": "(Vulgate) Asked what is the path, Yudhiṣṭhira answers: reasoning is unstable, the scriptures differ, there is no single sage whose view is authority; the truth of dharma is hidden in a cave — the path is that by which the great have gone.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "teacher-transmission"], "concepts": ["cpt:subtlety-of-dharma"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Not in the critical text (checked); vulgate verse number not confirmed.", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:vulg-3.313/3", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "vulg-3.313/3", "chapter": "vulg-3.313", "section": "Yakṣapraśna (vulgate only)"}, "paraphrase": "(Vulgate) Asked what the news is, Yudhiṣṭhira answers: in this cauldron of great delusion, with the sun as fire and days and nights as fuel, stirred by the ladle of months and seasons, Time cooks beings — that is the news.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "cosmic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["world-fate", "death-dying"], "concepts": ["cpt:time-as-destroyer"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Not in the critical text (checked); vulgate verse number not confirmed.", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:vulg-3.313/4", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "vulg-3.313/4", "chapter": "vulg-3.313", "section": "Yakṣapraśna (vulgate only)"}, "paraphrase": "(Vulgate) Asked who is happy, Yudhiṣṭhira answers: the one who, free of debt and not living abroad, cooks vegetables in his own house on the fifth or sixth day.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Not in the critical text (checked); vulgate verse number not confirmed.", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yaksaprasna:vulg-3.313/5", "source": "src:yaksaprasna", "location": {"ref": "vulg-3.313/5", "chapter": "vulg-3.313", "section": "Yakṣapraśna (vulgate only)"}, "paraphrase": "(Vulgate) Asked whether birth, conduct, study or learning makes a brāhmaṇa, Yudhiṣṭhira answers that conduct (vṛtta) alone does, not birth, study or learning.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics", "dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:varna-by-birth-or-conduct-epic"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U05-gita-epic"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "notes": "Not in the critical text (checked); the critical text has the parallel answer to Nahuṣa (3.177.16).", "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U05-gita-epic"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
